<DocPage eyebrow="CHANGELOG / V1.2.0" title="v1.2.0 — migration" lead="In v1.2.0 and later, remote automation requires an explicit build choice. Review this compatibility change before upgrading a v1.1.2 game integration." description="Gua v1.2.0 compatibility change: explicit remote build flag, ordinary release (no remote control) default OFF, fixed AI-player-enabled release ceiling, and package migration." section="changelog">
  <h2>Important compatibility change</h2>
  <p>Choose a purpose—ordinary release, development, testing, or an AI-player-enabled release—separately from Debug/Release configuration. A Release executable for ordinary players and one for remote testing need different purpose settings.</p>
  <p>Choosing Debug/Release, exporting a Release executable, or setting <code>GUA_OBSERVATION_PROFILE=player</code> does not enable remote automation. To enable remote control, native source builds require an explicit <code>GUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION=ON</code> together with an appropriate <code>GUA_DISTRIBUTION_FLAVOR</code>. The default is <code>shipping</code> / OFF, with the remote implementation excluded at compile/link/package time.</p>
  <ul><li>Development / Editor use (<code>development</code>): explicitly enable remote access when needed; local-only OFF is also supported.</li><li>Release build for testing: explicitly enable remote access with <code>qa</code>, with Debug as the maximum profile.</li><li>AI-player-enabled release: explicitly enable remote access with <code>ai-player</code>, with a fixed Player ceiling.</li><li>Ordinary release (no remote control, <code>shipping</code>): no remote implementation; runtime environment variables and APIs cannot restore it.</li></ul>
  <p>The access profile is another choice: Debug permits detailed inspection; Player permits only information and actions exposed to players. Neither profile adds remote code that was omitted from the build.</p>
  <h2>What to change when upgrading</h2>
  <ol><li>Choose the build purpose before installing/building the game integration.</li><li>Replace matched native/managed/add-on/test components together and use fresh build directories and separate directories for preparing the package.</li><li>For Unity source adapters, match <code>GUA_REMOTE_AUTOMATION</code> and, for an AI-player-enabled release, <code>GUA_PLAYER_ONLY</code>. These cannot alter a prebuilt native DLL or precompiled managed assembly.</li><li>Keep the manifests that record each packaged file’s build purpose, identity, and hashes, and retain those checks. Native NuGet pack requires verified per-RID assets; the unlabeled single-DLL fallback is removed.</li><li>Verify absence on ordinary release (no remote control), allowed Player operations without Debug elevation on an AI-player-enabled release, and remote operation on the testing/development build with remote access explicitly enabled.</li></ol>
  <p>Bridge ABI failure stubs and necessary local APIs remain. The development presets and SDK build workflows explicitly enable remote access; an SDK archive or “Release” name does not guarantee a build without remote control. This change does not automatically enable Player gameplay input or replace host exposure policy.</p>
  <p><a class="button" href="/remote-builds/">Required flags, commands, engine setup, and migration steps →</a></p>
</DocPage>
